  "what_happened_next": {
    "text": "Apple grew to nearly six times its starting value, far ahead of this set. Bonds gained more than the US share basket, and HP’s gain was much smaller than Apple’s. These companies all sold technology, but their shareholders had very different experiences through the financial crisis.\n\nLehman Brothers filed for bankruptcy in September 2008 during the financial crisis. The Deepwater Horizon disaster occurred in 2010. A devastating earthquake and tsunami struck Japan in 2011. Investors lived through these events month by month, without a complete picture of how the next one would affect their lives or investments.\n\nApple’s large gain can draw the eye away from the other choices. Yet HP and Microsoft were also major technology businesses. Their different outcomes show why an industry name is not enough to describe an investment. Owning many companies would have reduced reliance on any one of them, while also giving less weight to the company that happened to gain most.",
    "source_ids": [
      "french-us",
      "french-international",
      "fred-gs5",
      "yahoo-hpq",
      "yahoo-aapl",
      "yahoo-msft",
      "financial-crisis",
      "deepwater",
      "japan-quake"
    ]
  },
  "reflection": {
    "what_people_were_focused_on": "Computer makers were competing for home users, the new Fed chair weighed inflation, and diplomacy struggled with armed conflict.",
    "what_actually_mattered": "One industry label hid large differences between companies. The broad baskets spread those risks, while bonds followed a different path.",
    "what_faded_away": "A new shop, a delayed product and a strong quarter did not reveal the final ranking. Each was only part of a longer story.",
    "what_nobody_knew": "Nobody knew Apple’s eventual gain or the crisis ahead. Picking the winner now is easier than deciding among uncertain businesses then.",
